    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently reviewing the stability profiles for a new low-fat mayonnaise formulation. We're seeing some issues with phase separation in high-acid environments (pH < 3.8). Can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these high proton concentrations, and how the amphiphilic nature of the molecule helps prevent coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, being an amphiphilic phospholipid, orients its hydrophilic headgroups towards the aqueous phase and hydrophobic tails towards the oil droplets at the interface. Under high acidity, the protonation of the phosphate headgroups can alter electrostatic repulsion, but the steric hindrance provided by the adsorbed lecithin molecules remains crucial. These molecules form a rigid protective layer around the dispersed oil droplets, effectively creating a physical barrier that prevents droplets from coming close enough to overcome the repulsive forces and coalesce.",
      "source": "target",
